Spring's event system has three components. The **publisher** calls `applicationEventPublisher.publishEvent(event)`. The **`ApplicationEventMulticaster`** discovers all listeners registered for that event type. Each **listener** receives the event and processes it.

Since Spring 4.2, events can be any object — you don't need to extend `ApplicationEvent` anymore. And listeners are just methods annotated with `@EventListener`, making the wiring zero-boilerplate. The following example shows the complete event flow — a domain event POJO, a publishing service, and a consuming listener.

```java
// Event — plain POJO, no Spring dependency
public record OrderPlaced(String orderId, BigDecimal total) {}

// Publisher — injects Spring's event infrastructure
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher publisher;
    public OrderService(ApplicationEventPublisher publisher) {
        this.publisher = publisher;
    }
    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        repository.save(order);
        publisher.publishEvent(new OrderPlaced(order.getId(), order.getTotal()));
    }
}

// Listener — runs synchronously in the publisher's thread by default
@Component
public class InventoryListener {
    @EventListener
    public void onOrderPlaced(OrderPlaced event) {
        inventoryService.reserve(event.orderId());
    }
}
```

The listener runs on the same thread and in the same transaction as `placeOrder()`. If `reserve()` throws an exception, the entire transaction rolls back — including the `repository.save()`. This is often not what developers intended.

The production pattern for events that should only fire after a successful commit is **`@TransactionalEventListener`**. It binds the listener to a specific transaction phase — most commonly `AFTER_COMMIT` — so the event processing only happens if the transaction succeeds.

This solves the email notification problem. You don't want to send an 'Order Confirmed' email if the order transaction later rolls back due to a payment failure. With `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)`, the email only sends after the commit is final.

```java
@Component
public class NotificationListener {
    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void sendConfirmation(OrderPlaced event) {
        // Only runs if the transaction committed successfully
        emailService.send(event.orderId());
    }
}
```

There's a subtle trap: if you use `@TransactionalEventListener` and no transaction is active when the event is published, **the listener never fires** — silently. Spring doesn't throw an error; it just discards the event. Add `fallbackExecution = true` if you need the listener to fire even outside a transaction context.

For true **async event handling**, combine `@Async` with `@EventListener`. This runs the listener on a separate thread pool, fully decoupling execution. But `@Async` requires an `@EnableAsync` configuration and a properly sized thread pool — without it, tasks queue up on the default `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor` which creates unbounded threads.
